#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157040 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "1abed37b-99f8-5298-bc7b-f80259c78398" :question/math-academy-id "e-7446" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "af2757f8-0392-54c8-b681-162ee13b1804" :question/math-academy-id "e-7447" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "780170af-a92e-5a87-b08d-d9d940d6906b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7448" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad394608-abcb-5de2-b5ff-e06516eabe0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288355} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2c872a-7d0c-506f-9378-3d42b3eee454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288356} {:answer/id #uuid "835e0a1a-b723-5064-8e33-8987a3a18d7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288357} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecadcb3-f282-5d55-8bd9-2ec6296e661b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288358} {:answer/id #uuid "8e36d544-da4c-5b38-a3f0-cea6d0790f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad394608-abcb-5de2-b5ff-e06516eabe0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288355} :answer-field/id #uuid "904337e3-8d48-5969-92a8-aaf05b0b62b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fedc73e9-6bd7-5662-845d-cc5946c6553c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106447" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa57c197-4d9c-5c03-96f0-8a7bead8fcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288369} {:answer/id #uuid "76a74879-e62a-5db9-bd67-4c17732534c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288370} {:answer/id #uuid "b75efa1a-29cb-56b2-88d0-505084f1fd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=2t,\\,\\,y-3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288371} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f086ac-b5c1-584f-9053-363ffda12355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=2t,\\,\\,y+3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288372} {:answer/id #uuid "792a5ae4-4af3-59c8-84a1-7dc28471f787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=4t,\\,\\,y+3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa57c197-4d9c-5c03-96f0-8a7bead8fcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288369} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1e4cfb-2b12-591a-a043-70d77182305d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbfcd767-3d5f-5cb8-8eab-0b33a201040a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106455" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b885b18-3e83-562e-aa15-bede595287dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310393} {:answer/id #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf074c0-d547-57da-90fc-af45742a3986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310395} {:answer/id #uuid "5d878cd8-ea9d-571e-b2c4-2747cdeb4e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310396} {:answer/id #uuid "5aebc749-f346-5867-826e-d05adad1e8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} :answer-field/id #uuid "1938660b-7d05-579b-9d0b-397ac95ff0de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14e2d3b4-1c1a-54b9-9aa2-2d7f1398ec81" :question/math-academy-id "q-106461" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ea6cc9-037b-5caf-8807-ba3a131db9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ea6cc9-037b-5caf-8807-ba3a131db9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288422} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f15f65c-b5da-5827-843f-3ab9389d9944" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68c4bc08-c537-579b-bd5c-6f88aa96796f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c4bc08-c537-579b-bd5c-6f88aa96796f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288424} :answer-field/id #uuid "6304e4eb-2a97-5376-8811-3dcf21cea22e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d85e680-26c7-5aac-9193-74809aef6804" :question/math-academy-id "q-323229" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c31345-f3ee-5e7e-9b05-6b50cb831c66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y - 3)^{2} = 6(x - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155963} {:answer/id #uuid "240642c6-9520-558d-ba8a-d7eedcdbb8ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y - 3)^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}(x - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155964} {:answer/id #uuid "344383fc-d5d2-5ef6-99db-692a09c5acca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 6)^{2} = 24(y - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155965} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d5c20-b9cd-5208-9d70-febc48a8cb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 6)^{2} = 6(y - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155966} {:answer/id #uuid "78a34d11-c873-5428-bcf8-4c7baab3b4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 6)^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}(y - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b9d5c20-b9cd-5208-9d70-febc48a8cb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 6)^{2} = 6(y - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155966} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad12690-d1e7-50cd-b868-51b29fd4f0ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b1571ae-cba4-599c-b064-df002d19a987" :question/math-academy-id "q-84469" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-6}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1e61765-b80b-504d-9903-e65e40eb0e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310400} {:answer/id #uuid "d80fe1bd-97bd-5de8-bcf7-9febea4261cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310401} {:answer/id #uuid "859997d3-a529-5cab-a1c7-76920df6f3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310402} {:answer/id #uuid "c5566a4d-7138-557e-b675-c0824617b854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310403} {:answer/id #uuid "912c272a-14e2-5247-bd7e-23ee7b3ae2e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859997d3-a529-5cab-a1c7-76920df6f3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310402} :answer-field/id #uuid "190ef792-c922-5d09-9b81-5dfb13cfb364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1250f77b-d8f7-59d3-9273-1f7c3e0a3b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-84819" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a097a16-8ddf-52c7-a590-ae96ec43f4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310407} {:answer/id #uuid "5713448a-87f0-5942-a275-4f1cd9aaf1d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310408} {:answer/id #uuid "d3243e97-179a-552a-8f26-a1af3dedb265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=-3t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310409} {:answer/id #uuid "9305d5cc-2022-5d4f-b678-b75458e98e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310410} {:answer/id #uuid "f716a89c-c284-50cc-9c82-ba1826cd4167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a097a16-8ddf-52c7-a590-ae96ec43f4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310407} :answer-field/id #uuid "545cf95e-06ea-56fe-a594-ff692f74aec8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14a70bce-c4a4-5acc-ac6d-0383921a9b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84821" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/91500faad34245d5b2389ae71aa39f34b10b9a75b1b75307b5aa6231b311307b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(1,2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-4),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y-2=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ee5ddc-90cc-5e13-8422-546f3e0b2696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310414} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2cc450-47d9-525c-b902-ff52165199bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=-2t,\\,y-3=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310415} {:answer/id #uuid "906738ad-407b-570a-8047-fbc4a1ef44c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=-2t,\\,y+3=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310416} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd44e27-2480-5ca2-8900-037e49846439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2t,\\,y+3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310417} {:answer/id #uuid "62683e27-9171-5d90-bde1-8be606ab07d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=2t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4ee5ddc-90cc-5e13-8422-546f3e0b2696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310414} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f3662d-5e8e-5a85-9562-de183a36299b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09777cff-2521-5a71-be45-4262e5697b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84828" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-1,4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=2pt \\\\ 1=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(1)t,\\,y-3=(1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e627b7-bfd6-534e-830d-90b90bf24d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301498} {:answer/id #uuid "6598d4bd-0f51-59f8-96b1-480f3601275c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499} {:answer/id #uuid "809636ff-d992-572c-a491-dbcaab56b899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301500} {:answer/id #uuid "36b5d164-c361-542e-8465-94bac038821d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301501} {:answer/id #uuid "96e52685-a506-5142-9b93-9931257df6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6598d4bd-0f51-59f8-96b1-480f3601275c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499} :answer-field/id #uuid "6baca86a-7538-54f8-b930-9937030a4c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84bc7a7c-9e6f-5cc0-a103-68903527aa4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84840" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5cfbb25-241e-55fa-a7d4-89ae164d5aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310421} {:answer/id #uuid "668b71eb-4d27-5add-b663-e98d9703dc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310422} {:answer/id #uuid "6daa4abe-4ca9-5198-82b7-01d76b078024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310423} {:answer/id #uuid "9e016e23-f067-5f42-adf8-de94c24af360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310424} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dc9526-9759-5a8d-99ba-f52ca3e1c07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5cfbb25-241e-55fa-a7d4-89ae164d5aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310421} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f24eaa-bd31-5c6c-80a0-5608034660ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c9b11f-76b1-5474-bb7c-bc45490a8350" :question/math-academy-id "q-84842" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b76ff9a-20ce-58dd-bb5b-a2c91fe9986e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301505} {:answer/id #uuid "af9df707-eabb-52a0-bce2-cc41d484d781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301506} {:answer/id #uuid "c88ca086-1050-5d05-ac5b-1586a1b40966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301507} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a45b32-3143-507e-adbd-6eae182996ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=-{t}^{2},\\,y+2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301508} {:answer/id #uuid "fa501ae9-403a-52fc-b10a-a5abbc6d0417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c88ca086-1050-5d05-ac5b-1586a1b40966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301507} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc2f9049-4ae2-592c-b08f-435e027d0fcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23c1a911-6224-553e-84b2-80970be0d8e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-84843" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d42de3a7-da1a-503b-8937-62a5db43cc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310428} {:answer/id #uuid "24e4195f-4961-58a3-ad6d-927d4d45b309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310429} {:answer/id #uuid "d57b6a3b-d091-5774-918e-c8541446559a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310430} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed46ce7-b5e3-5fce-957f-c49ee73a0249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310431} {:answer/id #uuid "f6108aff-0558-5ff8-8ea2-e84e8090de7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+3=12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24e4195f-4961-58a3-ad6d-927d4d45b309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310429} :answer-field/id #uuid "010cbe17-2fb0-5d20-8c1d-0e8c93c87267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b6384ed-5b0e-577c-a6f0-3fed248e922b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84844" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15807e4f-e2b3-5836-9e8c-3f816ab663d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=16(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310435} {:answer/id #uuid "26306e22-7b41-5a1d-a185-58ae76b495f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=2(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310436} {:answer/id #uuid "8abf969a-44a9-5a46-89c7-c62aacde1abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=8(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310437} {:answer/id #uuid "837f3d92-4f98-5a73-8554-2b46459a5a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310438} {:answer/id #uuid "0d83e47e-f5a8-5692-9328-8b1dbf49f1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=2(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837f3d92-4f98-5a73-8554-2b46459a5a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310438} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d9b3bd0-0efd-5dd5-8533-fc68bb1b1ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58efaf59-7cd7-5488-89c6-5996c028fb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84853" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2{t}^{2},\\,y-4=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-4}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+3}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+3}{2} \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+3}{2}) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =8(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d0b6a3-4155-5e70-a0c7-78bc670f44d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288448} {:answer/id #uuid "38917827-6af0-5c38-b487-5c5e1726bac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288449} {:answer/id #uuid "670b7ebf-f3db-5ec2-8cab-43a4597d1678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-8(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288450} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6aaa2c-6bb8-5d0f-8dfb-ab79a6e5f6d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-2(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288451} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7d2afa-4da0-578d-99be-a65c3d8f3e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e7d2afa-4da0-578d-99be-a65c3d8f3e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288452} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c9ce65-48b0-577b-9094-c99c3fb2fe40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0acf6f7c-0905-523f-bd2c-b81ada5c183d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84854" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5397c54-8139-5733-8580-a4f79904c1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-8(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310442} {:answer/id #uuid "46c5c08d-b65f-5780-b59b-90ab99b37d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310443} {:answer/id #uuid "87e44316-19fe-540a-8356-0d990c5e2313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310444} {:answer/id #uuid "eb305ccf-145b-5207-8bd6-bdc9122f300d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310445} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9e5623-3783-565e-919b-bb799b142bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-4(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb305ccf-145b-5207-8bd6-bdc9122f300d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310445} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49ae9ed-1279-5d35-9aee-1192b1b696a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86db9fdb-2ad8-540e-aac1-953ec9683685" :question/math-academy-id "q-84858" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y-1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x-4}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-4}{2}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-8(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
